# Workflow: Parallel Execution

Purpose:
  Enable parallel execution of independent tasks in the SDDRA system.
  Improves performance by running multiple stages or tasks concurrently
  while maintaining dependency ordering.

## Parallelism Levels

### Stage-Level Parallelism
  Independent stages can run in parallel:
  - D1 (docs) and S1 (sdd) can run in parallel if docs are approved
  - BE, FE, MD can run in parallel after architecture is decided
  - QA, DO can run in parallel after code is ready

### Task-Level Parallelism
  Independent tasks can run in parallel:
  - Multiple features in different modules
  - Multiple bug fixes
  - Multiple documentation tasks

### Skill-Level Parallelism
  Independent skills can run in parallel:
  - L1 skills for different technologies
  - Research tasks
  - Analysis tasks

## Parallel Execution Rules

  [P1] Only independent tasks can run in parallel
  [P2] Dependencies must be resolved before parallel execution
  [P3] Shared resources must be locked during parallel access
  [P4] Results must be merged after parallel execution
  [P5] Failures in one parallel task do not affect others
  [P6] Human gates are not parallelizable

## Parallel Execution Model

Sequential Parts:
  D0 -> P1 -> D0 (intent analysis, must be sequential)
  D0 -> D1 -> D0 (docs, can be parallel with S1 after approval)
  D0 -> S1 -> D0 (sdd, can be parallel with D1 after approval)
  D0 -> C1 -> D0 (code, can be parallel across modules)
  D0 -> R1 -> D0 (review, must be sequential)
  D0 -> DEP1 -> D0 (deploy, must be sequential)

Parallel Parts:
  D1 + S1 (after P1, if docs approved)
  BE + FE + MD (after AR, if architecture decided)
  QA + DO (after BE+FE+MD, if code ready)
  Multiple features (if independent)

## Parallel Execution Workflow

  1. Analyze dependencies
  2. Identify parallelizable tasks
  3. Create execution plan
  4. Allocate resources
  5. Execute in parallel
  6. Monitor progress
  7. Merge results
  8. Resolve conflicts
  9. Continue sequentially

## Resource Sharing

Shared Resources:
  - .sdd/ files (read-only during parallel execution)
  - Decision ledger (locked during writes)
  - Token budget (shared across parallel tasks)
  - Context cache (shared, read-only)

Locking Strategy:
  - Read lock: multiple parallel tasks can read
  - Write lock: exclusive access for modifications
  - Lock timeout: 30s default
  - Deadlock detection: enabled

## Parallel Execution Patterns

Fan-Out:
  One task spawns multiple parallel tasks
  Example: D1 spawns docs for BE, FE, MD, QA, DO

Fan-In:
  Multiple parallel tasks merge into one
  Example: BE, FE, MD merge into R1

Map-Reduce:
  Map: parallel processing
  Reduce: sequential merging
  Example: Multiple features mapped, then reduced to single release

Pipeline:
  Sequential stages with parallel workers
  Example: P1 -> [D1, S1] parallel -> C1 -> R1 -> DEP1

## Parallel Execution Commands

/sdd-parallel:
  Execute multiple prompts in parallel
  Usage: /sdd-parallel "prompt-001" "prompt-002" "prompt-003"

/sdd-fanout:
  Fan-out execution from current stage
  Usage: /sdd-fanout --stage=C1 --tasks=BE,FE,MD

/sdd-merge:
  Merge parallel execution results
  Usage: /sdd-merge --tasks=BE,FE,MD

## Parallel Execution Metrics

Track:
  - Parallel tasks count
  - Parallel execution time
  - Speedup factor
  - Resource utilization
  - Conflict rate
  - Merge time

## Parallel Execution Safety

Safety Checks:
  [PE1] Dependencies resolved before parallel start
  [PE2] Shared resources locked during access
  [PE3] Results validated after merge
  [PE4] Conflicts detected and resolved
  [PE5] Failures isolated to individual tasks

Recovery:
  - If parallel task fails: retry individually
  - If merge fails: rollback to last consistent state
  - If deadlock detected: abort and restart

